  "original_html": "<section aria-labelledby=\"primary-heading\" class=\"flex w-full flex-1 flex-col h-full max-w-[1024px]\"><h1 id=\"primary-heading\" class=\"sr-only\">Home</h1><div><div class=\"max-w-[680px] m-auto px-1 flex flex-col gap-y-6\"><h1 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\">Joining the Conversation</h1><div class=\"border-b border-slate-200\"></div><div id=\"pm-1\" class=\"border-0 flex flex-col gap-y-4 ProseMirror\" data-context=\"doc\" data-input-id=\"pm-doc-html-0dc45f0d-b0c0-47a8-b466-bee354a6f6af\" data-editable=\"false\" data-progress=\"\" contenteditable=\"false\" translate=\"yes\"><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_-who-is-at-the-table\">🔎 Who is at the table?</h2><p><b>One way students can join a conversation in progress</b> is to be invited to a metaphorical table where they can “chat” with various stakeholders about an issue. Students read and process different viewpoints, then “pull up a chair” to the table and contribute their own voices to a multi-perspective argument. The abstract concept of a conversation among sources can seem more concrete when students follow the table metaphor to support their note-taking. This approach has been named the <b>“Atwoodian Table”</b> after its creator, Robin Atwood, a National Writing Project teacher-leader.</p><p><b>Check out the slideshow below, which provides an introduction to the Atwoodian Table</b> and a sample lesson you might use with your students. The lesson sequence features excerpts with different viewpoints on one issue: <i>Should fancy restaurants ban children?</i> Links are provided to the full-length texts for further exploration of the topic.</p><iframe src=\"https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/e/2PACX-1vTBJNerm-7YHORUFirOV76fkrHIQbqFo9_hRUaJZN73BO4XDefG7WjpNK68G7qZT3dnrjPBvzzcdL6b/embed?start=false&amp;loop=false&amp;delayms=3000\" height=\"569\" sandbox=\"allow-scripts allow-forms allow-same-origin allow-popups allow-popups-to-escape-sandbox\" title=\"Iframe for url: https://docs.google.com/presentation/d/e/2PACX-1vTBJNerm-7YHORUFirOV76fkrHIQbqFo9_hRUaJZN73BO4XDefG7WjpNK68G7qZT3dnrjPBvzzcdL6b/embed?start=false&amp;loop=false&amp;delayms=3000\" style=\"width: 100%;\" contenteditable=\"false\"></iframe><p><br class=\"ProseMirror-trailingBreak\"/></p><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_-atwoodian-table\">🔎 Atwoodian Table</h2><p><b>Experience the metaphor in action! Create your own Atwoodian Table</b> by working through the lesson sequence below, which features excerpts focused on the topic of Toddlers and Technology. As you read through the sources, you will engage and “hear” the conversations that authorities are already having about the topic. <a href=\"https://docs.google.com/document/d/1aj5w1wbJJ3bRQGs0ICNqzvPgYHB15TbDjkNpR4HoX9s/template/preview\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span><b>Click here to make a template.</b></span></a></p><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h_-atwoodian-table__h_toddlers-and-technology\">Toddlers and Technology</h3><p>1. In your Writer’s Notebook or on a separate sheet of paper, set up your Atwoodian Table note-taking tool:</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p>Draw a circle in the center of your paper to represent the table.</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>In the circle, write the topic: Toddlers and Technology.</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>Draw four chairs, one for each source below, and label the chairs with the source’s name: AAP, Dan Yang, Lucy Jo Palladino, and Urvashi Sen.</p></li></ul><p>2. As you read each excerpt in the table below, add a word balloon to your note-taker and summarize the claim and evidence of each source.</p><p>3. After considering the claims and evidence from each source, what is your own claim What evidence helped you arrive at your claim?</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p>Draw your chair at the table and write your name on it.</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>Add your own claim and evidence to your chair.</p></li></ul><h3 class=\"text-2xl font-semibold\" id=\"h_-atwoodian-table__h_sources\">Sources</h3><p><a href=\"https://www.healthychildren.org/English/family-life/Media/Pages/How-to-Make-a-Family-Media-Use-Plan.aspx\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>American Academy of Pediatrics</span></a> (AAP)</p><ul class=\"list-disc pl-6\"><li class=\"\"><p>Excessive media use has been associated with obesity, lack of sleep, school problems, aggression and other behavior issues. Limit entertainment screen time to less than one or two hours per day.</p></li><li class=\"\"><p>For children under 2, substitute unstructured play and human interaction for screentime. The opportunity to think creatively, problem solve and develop reasoning and motor skills is more valuable for the developing brain than passive media intake.</p></li></ul><p><a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2015/04/13/bringing-up-baby-on-the-iphone/technology-can-stimulate-the-infant-brain\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Dan D. Yang, Founder of VINCI Education</span></a></p><p>Interactive games on tablets or phones require an infant child to decide what to do or where to go, which is an active use of technology. When properly selected, these games get infants to slow down and think, thus making decisions and exercising the gears in their brains. If you really have to cook your dinner while keeping your infant occupied, interactive games can be a healthy distraction.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.shambhala.com/parenting-in-the-age-of-attention-snatchers-3340.html\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Lucy Jo Palladino, psychologist and author</span></a></p><p>Children today face a challenge to their cognitive control because of attention snatchers. These computers, tablets, smartphones, TV, and game consoles open new worlds of information, skill-building, and enjoyment for kids. But they do so by providing an endless stream of novelty and stimulation that captures their attention (involuntary attention). To build cognitive control, a child needs to exercise effort to pay attention (voluntary attention). If she depends on a screen to pay attention, your child is weakening this essential ability.</p><p><a href=\"https://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2015/04/13/bringing-up-baby-on-the-iphone/how-i-found-a-happy-medium-in-exposing-my-son-to-the-ipad\" class=\"underline\" target=\"_blank\"><span>Urvashi Sen, Working Mom</span></a></p><p>Technology should be used with babies and children as a learning tool. I accept that it will be a huge part of my son’s life, and so I focus on teaching him how to use it in an active way that allows him to engage and interact – and no more than once or twice a week. There are several iPad apps that I credit with helping him learn his ABCs and how to count.</p><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_-join-the-discussion\">💬 Join the Discussion</h2><div class=\"grid place-items-center\" contenteditable=\"false\"><div class=\"relative flex flex-row justify-between group w-full\" id=\"image-view-736d474d-05aa-4f73-90ab-c2f7bc30e6e7\" style=\"width: 268.75px;\"><a data-type=\"image-ref\" data-width=\"43.00595238095239\" class=\"self-center w-full\"><img src=\"https://s3.amazonaws.com/com.appolearning.files/production/uploads/uploaded_file/76f13a11-ebca-4ac7-b59c-5281cf299faa/Discussion_icon_2022_copy_small.png\" class=\"w-full\"/></a></div></div><p>When you finish with Toddlers and Technology, <b>take a picture of your completed table and share it in the discussion. </b>Include a comment or two about your experience as well as your thoughts about how you might use this in your own classroom.</p><p>Be sure to respond to the posts of others!</p><p><br class=\"ProseMirror-trailingBreak\"/></p><h2 class=\"text-4xl font-bold\" data-version=\"0\" id=\"h_-earn-recognition\">🏅Earn Recognition</h2><div data-type=\"badge-embed\" data-badge-id=\"90eec2e7-21e5-4a5a-9918-83641aed4274\" data-badge-type=\"micro_credential\" contenteditable=\"false\"><a href=\"https://participate.community/badge/90eec2e7-21e5-4a5a-9918-83641aed4274\" class=\"flex flex-col border border-slate-300\"><div class=\"font-bold bg-slate-300 h-6 w-full pl-4\">Micro Credential</div><div class=\"flex justify-between\"><div class=\"p-4 flex flex-col justify-between\"><p class=\"font-bold line-clamp-1\">Reading to Write: Say-Mean-Matter</p><p class=\"text-slate-600 line-clamp-2\">This microcredential recognizes your planning and preparation to use the Say-Mean-Matter framework and/or They Say / I Say with your students. By reflecting on how you will introduce the routine, creating a classroom-ready example, and identifying which strategy you’ll try out in Module 4, you’ll take the next step in helping students move from summary toward deeper analysis and significance.</p><p class=\"text-slate-400 text-sm\">participate.community</p></div><img class=\"h-32 w-32 border-l border-slate-300 object-cover\" src=\"https://participate.community/img/k/public/5924276-Module%203%20Community%20Cultivating%20Literacy.png\"/></div></a></div><p><br class=\"ProseMirror-trailingBreak\"/></p></div><div id=\"doc-capture-source\" class=\"hidden\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></div><ul class=\"flex flex-col absolute border-2 rounded-md bg-white outline-0 z-20 text-slate-500\" id=\"slash-menu\" tabindex=\"0\" style=\"display: none;\"><li class=\"group flex gap-2 cursor-pointer p-2 items-center text-slate-600 focus:bg-slate-100 focus:text-slate-900 focus-visible:outline-none hidden\" tabindex=\"-1\" id=\"slash-menu-h2\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" fill=\"none\" viewbox=\"0 0 24 24\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke=\"currentColor\" class=\"size-6 stroke-slate-400 group-focus:stroke-slate-500\"><path stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" d=\"M21.75 19.5H16.5v-1.609a2.25 2.25 0 0 1 1.244-2.012l2.89-1.445c.651-.326 1.116-.955 1.116-1.683 0-.498-.04-.987-.118-1.463-.135-.825-.835-1.422-1.668-1.489a15.202 15.202 0 0 0-3.464.12